## Remarks

The compactness proof is independent of any Morse–Smale compactness supplier. Its uniform fold estimate is $\operatorname{dist}(f_s,D_s)\le C|\nabla f_s|^{3/2}$, giving height-curve equicontinuity $C\delta^{1/3}$ even when a Morse eigenvalue tends to zero.

The exact cubic gradient at the newborn Morse points has center eigenvalues $\pm6\rho$, not $\pm2$. The proof normalizes the local model only where a same-index passage is displayed, preserving the stable and unstable sections and the jet of the displayed endpoint incidence. Transit maps may change; step 8.1 excludes new nonpositive-index events by a shrinking-support broken-orbit argument.

A generic positive-drop trajectory space need not be slice-wise transverse at every parameter: for index drop one its one-dimensional total space may have critical points of projection to the parameter. The elementary attaching maps stay smooth through those tangencies by the bounded-time one-critical-band argument.

The incoming patch is a sphere patch of dimension $i-1$, with one parameter direction, and the lower stable section has codimension $i$. Isolate a compact orbit segment and entry patch around its single incidence point. In an already based representation for which the remaining attaching data have been kept out of that passage neighborhood, the displayed exit map in the [[lem-isolated-same-index-trajectory-passage-is-a-framed-handle-slide]] identifies the two sides with the framed band sum. Its full normal thickening and actual outgoing map are retained. Isolation from all additional based data, and the parameterwise reduction to such based representations, are conditions on the subsequent global normalization; they are not fabricated by this generic-field argument.

The construction above supplies the weak generic descending family, its finite nonpositive-index incidence set, its birth compactness analysis, and the framed ELEMENTARY attaching-map assertion. It specifically does not supply slice-wise Morse–Smale regularity at all positive drops. A claim that mere parameter transversality implies that stronger property would be false: the one-dimensional total moduli space for index drop one can have critical points of its projection to the parameter.

The remaining global obligation is precise: build the parameterwise identification of arbitrary based/index-grouped handle presentations with these elementary descriptions, isolate the framed passage neighborhoods relative to all other based attaching data, and show its transitions are generated by the allowed slides/trades/cancellations with the retained terminal map. Pointwise index ordering is weaker than that parameterwise naturality statement. That obligation belongs to the separate relative-Cerf/trade constructor and must not be declared discharged by this proposal or by the existing local slide computation.
